1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,309
YA-NEC video course - lesson 2: single-element wire antennas.

2
00:00:04,359 --> 00:00:11,842
YA-NEC course, lesson 2: single-element wire antennas. Everything starts from the Calculators tab.

3
00:00:11,892 --> 00:00:21,538
Dipoles, type Inverted V: here at 7.1 MHz, apex at 12 m, 120 degrees between the legs.

4
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:32,559
The real-time calculation below runs the NEC on every change: SWR 1.42, 5.36 dBi, maximum at 62 degrees - an NVIS-friendly antenna.

5
00:00:32,609 --> 00:00:45,194
Delta loop: since 1.0.11 the card has three sections. Dimensions: frequency 14.150 MHz, lowest point at 8 m. Shape: equilateral, apex up.

6
00:00:45,244 --> 00:00:57,194
Balun / feed point: each tile shows where the balun goes and the polarization it gives. Lambda/4 along the side: vertical. Back to the centre of the horizontal side.

7
00:00:57,244 --> 00:01:07,259
'Bring to resonance (NEC)'.

8
00:01:07,309 --> 00:01:18,768
Perimeter 22.63 m, sides 7.54 m. The NEC confirms about 120 ohm: SWR 2.4 on 50 ohm, too high.

9
00:01:18,818 --> 00:01:24,834
Open as model: the delta goes into the 3D structure.

10
00:01:24,884 --> 00:01:31,636
120.2 ohm, 7.19 dBi at 28 degrees, horizontal polarization.

11
00:01:31,686 --> 00:01:44,121
The classic remedy: a quarter wave of 75 ohm coax. Calculators, RG cables, frequency 14.150 MHz.

12
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:49,122
'from model' takes the impedance of the open model: 120 ohm.

13
00:01:49,172 --> 00:02:05,780
Ideal Z0 = sqrt(120 x 50) = 77.5 ohm: RG-59 is perfect. Cut length 3.50 m (VF 0.66), SWR 1.05, loss 0.16 dB.

14
00:02:05,830 --> 00:02:12,857
'Put the line in the open model (TL)': the cable becomes a NEC transmission line.

15
00:02:12,907 --> 00:02:20,779
Now the model, cable included, shows 46.8 ohm and SWR 1.07: the delta loop is matched.

16
00:02:20,829 --> 00:02:27,160
Verticals: lambda/4 with radials on the ground, 7.1 MHz, 16 radials.

17
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:39,178
10.04 m radiator. With 16 radials the ground loss is about 15 ohm: efficiency 71 % (ON4UN table 9-1), impedance about 51 ohm.

18
00:02:39,228 --> 00:02:43,252
5/8 wave for 145 MHz.

19
00:02:43,302 --> 00:02:51,640
1.228 m whip and a 602 nH coil at the base: more gain at low angle than the lambda/4.

20
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:56,275
Coil-loaded vertical: 6 m whip for 3.6 MHz.

21
00:02:56,325 --> 00:03:04,663
32 uH at the base, but only 13 % efficiency: a short vertical on 80 m pays a lot in coil and ground losses.

22
00:03:04,713 --> 00:03:14,273
J-pole for 145 MHz: 147.3 cm and 49.1 cm, feed 32 mm from the bottom.

23
00:03:14,323 --> 00:03:20,851
SWR 1.35. Warning: without a common-mode choke the coax radiates too.

24
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:30,318
Last one: the multiband vertical like the DX Commander. 'Check all bands' runs a sweep on every band with all elements present.

25
00:03:30,368 --> 00:04:35,212
Each band is computed in the background, about ten seconds each: you can keep working in the other tabs.

26
00:04:35,262 --> 00:04:44,745
The table: minimum SWR, frequency, gain at 10 degrees, efficiency, and which element 'steals' current from the one in resonance.

27
00:04:44,795 --> 00:04:51,853
On 20 m, for example, the 30 m wire takes 35 % of the current: trim one element at a time.

28
00:04:51,903 --> 00:04:59,950
Inverted V, delta loop with its quarter-wave transformer, verticals: all from the calculators. 73 de IC8TEM
